": "it", "name": "Italian"}, "word": "unico"}, {"language": {"code": "pdt", "name": "Plautdietsch"}, "word": "leef"}, {"language": {"code": "tr", "name": "Turkish"}, "word": "değerli"}, {"language": {"code": "tr", "name": "Turkish"}, "word": "kıymetli"}, {"language": {"code": "ota", "name": "Ottoman Turkish"}, "word": "عزیز"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(derogatory, antiphrastic) Treated with too much reverence.", "tags": ["derogatory"], "examples": ["He spent hours painting the eyes of the portrait, which his fellow artists regarded as a bit precious."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(writing, ironic) Excessively complicated.", "tags": ["ironic"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(informal, followed by about) Extremely protective or strict (about something).", "tags": ["informal"], "examples": ["Writers are often very precious about their work."], "quotes": [{"text": "Pro chefs can be very precious about their kit. Watch a bartender trying to borrow a simple, cheap fruit-knife from the kitchen and you'll see what I mean.", "reference": "2009 September 16, Charlie Sorrel, “Chef’s Travel Bag: A Kitchen On Your Back”, in Wired, San Francisco, Calif.: Condé Nast Publications, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 24 Mar 2023:"}, {"text": "Well, I didn't realize it until almost after the fact. I wrote all these songs very quickly; I did a whole lot of material and wasn't too precious about it. The lyric writing was done in much the same way. I wrote stuff and sang it, and the demos stuck, which is different from what I've done before, when I edited it.", "reference": "2016 February 4, Spencer Kornhaber, quoting Jeremy Greenspan, “The Postindustrial Electronic Bar-Fly Blues”, in The Atlantic, Washington, D.C.: The Atlantic Monthly Group, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 02 Oct 2022:"}, {"text": "Hearing what a struggle it was for her, I began to understand why some people are so precious about being Irish. Being second-generation Irish, she had the option to hide behind her accent. Instead she would thump her fist to defend Irish people from those speaking out against them.", "reference": "2016 November 20, Katie Walsh, “Why are Irish people so precious about Irishness?”, in The Irish Times, Dublin: Irish Times Trust, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 01 Apr 2023:"}, {"text": "The course is not precious about overly academic sources, either. ¶ \"The students are confused when I tell them to try and trace something down with a quick Wikipedia search, because they've been told not to do it,\" she [Christina Ladam] said. \"Not for research papers, but if you're trying to find out if a site is legitimate or if somebody has a history as a conspiracy theorist and you show them how to follow the page's citation, it's quick and effective, which means it's more likely to be used.\"", "reference": "2021 February 18, Charlie Warzel, “Don't Go Down the Rabbit Hole”, in The New York Times, New York, N.Y.: The New York Times Company, →ISSN, →OCLC, archived from the original on 27 Jul 2023:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(informal, derogatory) Blasted; damned.", "tags": ["derogatory", "informal"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "It’s all owing to your precious caution that they got hold of it. If you had let me burn it, and taken my word that it was gone, it would have been a heap of ashes behind the fire, instead of being whole and sound, inside of my great-coat.", "reference": "1839, Charles Dickens, Nicholas Nickleby:"}], "synonyms": ["confounded", "ever-loving", "raasclaat", "4ucking", "accursed", "bally", "blasted", "bleeding", "bleeping", "blessed", "blimming", "blinking", "bloody", "blooming", "confounded", "consarned", "cotton-picking", "cowson", "cursed", "dadburn", "dadgum", "dagnabbed", "